README

This library provides a PID controller for closed-loop control applications
(e.g. temperature, motor speed or position control) and a relay feedback
auto-tuner, which determines suitable controller gains automatically.

The library does not use the math library or dynamic memory allocation and can
be used on all Raspberry Pi models. Build it with:

	make clean
	make

This generates the library libpid.a. Add it to the LIBS of your application:

	LIBS = $(CIRCLEHOME)/addon/pid/libpid.a ...

and include the headers with:

	#include <pid/pidcontroller.h>
	#include <pid/pidautotuner.h>

CPIDController
--------------

Discrete PID controller in parallel form:

	u = Kp * (b*r - y) + Ki * integral (r - y) dt + Kd * d/dt (c*r - y) + FF

r: setpoint, y: measurement, b, c: setpoint weights, FF: feed-forward.
Ki = Kp/Ti (1/s), Kd = Kp*Td (s).

Features:

* Fixed sample time (Update (r, y)) or variable sample time (Update (r, y, dt))
* Output limits with anti-windup (conditional integration or back-calculation)
* Derivative on measurement by default (no "derivative kick" on setpoint steps)
* First-order low-pass filter for the derivative term
* Setpoint weighting for the proportional and derivative term
* Output rate limit (slew rate)
* Integration deadband
* Direct acting (e.g. heater) or reverse acting (e.g. cooler)
* Manual mode with bumpless transfer to automatic mode
* Bumpless start from an existing actuator output (Initialize ())
* Gain changes during operation without output bump
* Access to the P, I and D terms for diagnostics

Typical usage (100 Hz loop):

	CPIDController PID (2.0f, 0.5f, 0.1f, 0.01f);	// Kp, Ki, Kd, sample time
	PID.SetOutputLimits (0.0f, 100.0f);
	PID.SetDerivativeFilter (0.05f);

	// every 10 ms:
	float fOutput = PID.Update (fSetpoint, ReadSensor ());
	WriteActuator (fOutput);

The controller is not thread-safe. Call all methods of one instance from the
same execution context (task, core or interrupt handler).

CPIDAutoTuner
-------------

Relay feedback auto-tuner (Astrom-Hagglund). While running it replaces the
controller and toggles the output between Base-Step and Base+Step around the
setpoint. The process oscillates with its ultimate period Tu. From the period
and the amplitude of this oscillation the ultimate gain Ku is calculated and
the PID gains are derived using one of these tuning rules:

	Ziegler-Nichols PI / PID
	Tyreus-Luyben PI / PID	(less overshoot, more robust)
	Some overshoot PID
	No overshoot PID

The first oscillation cycle is ignored, the following cycles are averaged. The
hysteresis should be larger than the noise of the measurement. The process must
tolerate the output swing during the tuning.

	CPIDAutoTuner Tuner;
	Tuner.Start (50.0f, 40.0f, 30.0f, 0.2f);	// setpoint, base, step, hysteresis

	while (Tuner.IsRunning ())			// every sample time
	{
		WriteActuator (Tuner.Update (ReadSensor (), 0.01f));
	}

	if (Tuner.GetState () == CPIDAutoTuner::StateFinished)
	{
		Tuner.ApplyTunings (CPIDAutoTuner::RuleTyreusLuybenPID, &PID);
	}

See sample/52-pidcontrol for a complete example.
